Clearing the interval in the `useEffect` return is necessary, but it is not sufficient if the effect also subscribes to a WebSocket.

A correct cleanup should usually do both:

```tsx
useEffect(() => {
  const socket = new WebSocket(url)

  const intervalId = window.setInterval(() => {
    // periodic work
  }, 1000)

  socket.onmessage = (event) => {
    // handle message
  }

  return () => {
    clearInterval(intervalId)

    socket.onmessage = null
    socket.onerror = null
    socket.onclose = null

    if (
      socket.readyState === WebSocket.OPEN ||
      socket.readyState === WebSocket.CONNECTING
    ) {
      socket.close()
    }
  }
}, [url])
```

So: if your cleanup only has `clearInterval(...)`, then the WebSocket can remain open after unmount and continue receiving events. That would be a memory/resource leak.

Also check the dependency array. If the socket URL or subscription inputs change, they should be dependencies so React cleans up the old socket before creating a new one.

